{
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1-v2",
      "text": "What is a list?",
      "options": [
        "Many values in one variable",
        "Many variables made on one line",
        "A loop that goes over many values",
        "Text with the values split by commas"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "text": "Which brackets make a list?",
      "options": ["( )", "[ ]", "{ }", "< >"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q3-v2",
      "text": "What goes between the elements of a list?",
      "options": ["A dot", "A semicolon", "A comma", "A space"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "text": "What index does the first element of a list have?",
      "options": ["1", "It has no index", "-1", "0"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "text": "`names = [\"Tommy\", \"Lance\", \"Cortez\"]`. Which one is \"Lance\"?",
      "options": ["names[1]", "names[2]", "names(1)", "names[Lance]"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q6-v2",
      "text": "What happens when this runs?",
      "code": "names = [\"Tommy\", \"Lance\", \"Cortez\"]\nprint(names[10])",
      "options": [
        "It prints Cortez, the last one",
        "It stops with an IndexError",
        "It prints nothing and goes on",
        "It prints 10, the number in brackets"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q7-v2",
      "text": "What does len() tell you about a list?",
      "options": [
        "How many letters its elements have",
        "The index of its last element",
        "How many elements it has",
        "The sum of the numbers in it"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q8",
      "text": "What does .append() do?",
      "options": [
        "It adds an element at the start",
        "It replaces the first element",
        "It sorts the list",
        "It adds an element at the end"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q9-v2",
      "text": "After `names.append(\"Cortez\")`, what is names?",
      "options": [
        "The same list, with Cortez at the end",
        "Unchanged; append gave back a new list",
        "A new list that holds only \"Cortez\"",
        "The same list, with Cortez at the start"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q10",
      "text": "What does the second line do?",
      "code": "names = [\"Tommy\", \"Lance\", \"Cortez\"]\nnames[1] = \"Ken\"",
      "options": [
        "It adds Ken at the end",
        "It puts Ken in place of Lance",
        "It puts Ken in place of Tommy",
        "It stops with an error"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q11-v2",
      "text": "Which of these is a list of numbers?",
      "options": ["[\"42\", \"35\", \"60\"]", "\"42, 35, 60\"", "[42, 35, 60]", "[42 35 60]"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q12-v2",
      "text": "Can a list hold True and False?",
      "options": [
        "No, a list holds only text or numbers",
        "Yes, but only if they are in quotes",
        "No, True and False only work in an if",
        "Yes, a list holds any kind of value"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q13",
      "text": "A list has 5 elements. What is the index of the last one?",
      "options": ["4", "5", "6", "0"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q14-v2",
      "text": "Why is a list better than name1, name2, ... name100?",
      "options": [
        "Variable names can't have numbers in them",
        "One variable holds all of them, however many",
        "A list makes the program run much faster",
        "In a list, the names can't be changed later"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q15",
      "text": "Which line adds \"Cortez\" at the end of names?",
      "options": [
        "names.add(\"Cortez\")",
        "names + \"Cortez\"",
        "names.append(\"Cortez\")",
        "append(names, \"Cortez\")"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q16",
      "text": "Which line makes \"Tommy\" the first element of names?",
      "options": [
        "names[1] = \"Tommy\"",
        "names(0) = \"Tommy\"",
        "names = \"Tommy\"",
        "names[0] = \"Tommy\""
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q17",
      "text": "Which of these is an empty list?",
      "options": ["[]", "[0]", "\"\"", "[\"\"]"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q18-v2",
      "text": "What do we call the 2 in `names[2]`?",
      "options": ["The length", "The index", "The element", "The parameter"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q19-v2",
      "text": "How is .append() on a list unlike .upper() on a string?",
      "options": [
        "append gives back a new list, upper changes the string",
        "Both change the variable itself, nothing new is made",
        "append changes the list, upper gives back a new string",
        "Both give back a new one and leave the old one alone"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q20-v2",
      "text": "Can one list mix numbers, text and True?",
      "options": [
        "No, Python stops with an error when you mix them",
        "Numbers and text can mix, but not with True",
        "Yes, and it's the most common way to use a list",
        "Yes, but it's rare and often a sign of messy design"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q21-v2",
      "text": "Is len(names) the index of the last element?",
      "options": [
        "No, the last index is len(names) - 1",
        "Yes, the last index is len(names)",
        "No, the last index is len(names) + 1",
        "They have nothing to do with each other"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q22",
      "text": "In the Cargo Bay mission, which line empties the item at index 1?",
      "options": [
        "cargo[2] = \"empty\"",
        "cargo[1] = \"empty\"",
        "cargo.append(\"empty\")",
        "cargo = \"empty\""
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q23",
      "text": "In the Cargo Bay mission, after the 4 items are added, what is len(cargo)?",
      "options": ["3", "5", "4", "0"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q24-v2",
      "text": "What happens to Lance after `names[1] = \"Ken\"` in `[\"Tommy\", \"Lance\"]`?",
      "options": [
        "He moves one place right, after Ken",
        "He stays, and Ken is added at the end",
        "Python stops: index 1 already has a value",
        "He is gone, replaced by Ken at index 1"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q25",
      "text": "Which of these is not a list?",
      "options": ["\"Tommy, Lance\"", "[\"Tommy\", \"Lance\"]", "[]", "[1, 2]"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q26",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "names = [\"Tommy Vercetti\", \"Lance Vance\"]\nprint(names[0])",
      "options": ["'Tommy Vercetti'", "Tommy Vercetti", "['Tommy Vercetti']", "T"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q27-v2",
      "text": "A list has 3 elements. Can you read the one at index 3?",
      "options": [
        "Yes, the third one sits at index 3",
        "Yes, it starts over and gives the first",
        "No, its indexes are only 0, 1 and 2",
        "Yes, it gives back an empty string"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q28-v2",
      "text": "In what order are the elements after several appends?",
      "options": [
        "Newest first, each append goes in front",
        "In an order Python picks, it can change",
        "In alphabetical order, whatever you added",
        "In the order they were added, oldest first"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q29-v2",
      "text": "What does `len([])` give?",
      "options": ["0", "1", "An error", "2"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q30-v2",
      "text": "How does Python show a list of text when it prints the whole list?",
      "options": [
        "Each on its own line: Ana, then Ben",
        "With brackets and quotes: ['Ana', 'Ben']",
        "With commas but no brackets: Ana, Ben",
        "Joined with no spaces, like AnaBen"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    }
